WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.319 --> 00:00:06.200
One hallmark of writing good code is ensuring
an app does not crash due to a runtime error.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.080
Many programming languages, Python included,
have keywords we can use to attempt to run

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:16.740
code, control the error message displayed
if the code would otherwise cause a crash,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.630
and then run certain logic depending on whether
the original intent to run code worked.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:27.720
This concept of controlling errors and their
messages is known as exception handling.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:35.090
To start this exception handling process,
we have a project in this 521-try file, where,

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:41.969
on line 1, we have a list of figuratives for
language, five to be exact.

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:52.390
And then, on line 3, we have code to get someone
to input a number from 1 to 5 to get further

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:55.640
information on one of the five figuratives.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:05.150
On line 6, I have a formatted print statement
to print the figurative someone requested.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:11.520
On line four, we assign the number to the
index value of whatever figurative was chosen.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.549
Why the -1?

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:20.560
Remember that lists are zero-based, so these
five figuratives have an index of 0 through

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.079
4.

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:26.110
Asking someone to type that in could confuse
one trying to run the app.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:32.320
Thus, we can have one enter a number 1 through
5 and then subtract 1 from that entry to fit

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.110
the parameters of our zero-based list.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:43.270
I will run the code and enter a legitimate
number.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.090
That code works as expected.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:53.490
Next, I will rerun the code and enter a bad
value.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:02:02.490
The result returns an index out of range error,
known as an IndexError, as the number we submitted

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:05.579
is out of the range of items in the list.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:11.420
While that error message is descriptive, it
will not make sense to most, hence the need

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.739
to control an error message.

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:19.069
In this case, the first step in exception
handling is to add a try block to the code

00:02:19.069 --> 00:02:22.480
we are trying to run, lines 3 and 4.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:39.319
On line 2, I will type try and a colon, and
then indent lines 3 and 4 so that they sit

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:40.440
in this try block.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.220
A try block cannot run by itself, so we will
not run the code here.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:51.989
It's important to note that a try block usually
has at least an except block to catch exceptions

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:57.709
and often includes a finally block to execute
code regardless of whether an exception occurs.

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:00.620
Sometimes, it also includes an else block.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:06.120
However, a try block cannot run without at
least a finally or except block accompanying

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.120
it.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.980
The try block tells the app to run a code
block, hopefully without errors.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:18.019
In the following two topics, we will see how
to account for an error within the try block

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:20.590
and when the code in a try block causes no
errors.

